    wh:isInDanger true ;
    wh:isTransboundary false ;
    wh:locatedIn wh:country_CD ;
    wh:shortDescription "The property, located in the north-eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, forms part of one of the last extensive and continuous forest–savanna mosaic landscapes in Central Africa. Representing a major ecological transition zone between the Guineo-Congolian forest biome and the Sudanian savanna, it encompasses a diverse range of habitats, including grasslands, wooded savannas, semi-deciduous forests, rivers, gallery forests, marshes, and papyrus wetlands. Ecological processes such as fire, herbivory, and seasonal flooding continue to shape the landscape and maintain its forest–savanna dynamics. The park supports a rich assemblage of wildlife, including significant populations of African Forest Elephants, Kordofan Giraffes, Common Hippopotamuses, African buffaloes, lions, pangolins, and numerous primate species. It is home to more than 150 mammal species, 459 bird species, 83 reptile species, and 43 amphibian species, including threatened and regional endemic species."@en ;
    wh:unescoId 136 ;
    wh:uuid "b3b4d146-e827-554d-a09f-4f8781618cfa"^^xsd:string .

wh:site_1360 a wh:WorldHeritageSite ;
    rdfs:label "Nord-Pas de Calais Mining Basin"@en,
        "Cuenca minera de la región Nord-Pas de Calais"@es,
        "Bassin minier du Nord-Pas de Calais"@fr ;
    wh:areaHectares 3943.0 ;
    wh:belongsToRegion wh:region_EUR ;
    wh:description "Remarkable as a landscape shaped over three centuries of coal extraction from the 1700s to the 1900s, the site consists of 109 separate components over 120,000 ha. It features mining pits (the oldest of which dates from 1850) and lift infrastructure, slag heaps (some of which cover 90 ha and exceed 140 m in height), coal transport infrastructure, railway stations, workers’ estates and mining villages including social habitat, schools, religious buildings, health and community facilities, company premises, owners and managers’ houses, town halls and more. The site bears testimony to the quest to create model workers’ cities from the mid 19th century to the 1960s and further illustrates a significant period in the history of industrial Europe. It documents the living conditions of workers and the solidarity to which it gave rise."@en ;
