wh:site_1757 a wh:WorldHeritageSite ;
    rdfs:label "العاصمتان القديمتان أسوكا وفوجيوارا"@ar,
        "Ancient Capitals of Asuka and Fujiwara"@en,
        "Antiguas capitales de Asuka y Fujiwara"@es,
        "Anciennes capitales d’Asuka et de Fujiwara"@fr,
        "Древние столицы Асука и Фудзивара"@ru,
        "飞鸟与藤原古都"@zh ;
    wh:areaHectares 137.99 ;
    wh:belongsToRegion wh:region_APA ;
    wh:description "Located in the southern part of the Nara Basin, Asuka and Fujiwara were the two ancient capitals of the Japanese archipelago that marked the establishment of a centralised state system modelled after the Chinese codified law known as ritsuryō. Nineteen component parts, comprising palaces/government offices, Buddhist temples and mounded tombs, contain archaeological evidence of the Asuka Capital (588-694 CE) and the Fujiwara Capital (694-710 CE). The Asuka capital was established at the site of the Asuka-dera Temple, the first Buddhist temple in Japan. Together, the sites illustrate the introduction and consolidation of key elements of early centralized governance and influenced the later Japanese capitals of Nara and Kyoto."@en ;
    wh:hasCategory wh:category_Cultural ;
    wh:hasComponent wh:component_1757-001,
