    wh:isTransboundary false ;
    wh:locatedIn wh:country_JP ;
    wh:shortDescription "Located in the southern part of the Nara Basin, Asuka and Fujiwara were the two ancient capitals of the Japanese archipelago that marked the establishment of a centralised state system modelled after the Chinese codified law known as ritsuryō. Nineteen component parts, comprising palaces/government offices, Buddhist temples and mounded tombs, contain archaeological evidence of the Asuka Capital (588-694 CE) and the Fujiwara Capital (694-710 CE). The Asuka capital was established at the site of the Asuka-dera Temple, the first Buddhist temple in Japan. Together, the sites illustrate the introduction and consolidation of key elements of early centralized governance and influenced the later Japanese capitals of Nara and Kyoto."@en ;
    wh:unescoId 1757 ;
    wh:uuid "5ee7b890-1af5-5c58-a796-5e783c53289a"^^xsd:string .

wh:site_1758 a wh:WorldHeritageSite ;
    rdfs:label "أحافير الأسماك العظمية في بحر ليمفيورد الغربي – التطور والتكيف مع المناخ في بدايات العصر الإيوسيني"@ar,
        "The Bony Fish Fossils of the Western Limfjord – Evolution and Climate Adaptation in the Earliest Eocene"@en,
        "Los fósiles de peces óseos de Limfjord occidental: evolución y adaptación climática en el Eoceno inferior"@es,
        "Les fossiles de poissons osseux du Limfjord occidental – évolution et adaptation climatique au cours de l’Éocène inférieur"@fr,
        "Ископаемые остатки костистых рыб Западного Лим-фьорда: эволюция и адаптация к климату в начале эоцена"@ru,
        "利姆水道西段硬骨鱼化石——始新世最早期的演化和气候适应"@zh ;
    wh:areaHectares 7.0 ;
    wh:belongsToRegion wh:region_EUR ;
    wh:description "The serial property, located in the Western Limfjord sea of northern Jutland, Denmark, comprise the coastal cliffs of Hanklit and Knudeklint, which contain some of the world’s best-preserved fossil fishes. The property represents the oldest and most important site for understanding the early evolution and diversification of modern bony fishes (teleosts) following the end-Cretaceous mass extinction. Preserved within diatomite-rich deposits and volcanic ash layers, the exceptional fossil record spans the Paleocene–Eocene Thermal Maximum (PETM) and the subsequent cooling period, providing unique evidence of biotic responses to major climatic change. The property contains abundant and exceptionally well-preserved marine and terrestrial fossils, including fishes, sea turtles, birds, insects, plants, and microfossils, offering an unparalleled insight into the earliest Eocene biosphere. It preserves between 25 and 29 fish orders and more than 80 fish species, documenting the evolution and adaptation of early modern fish lineages during a period of rapid environmental change."@en ;
    wh:hasCategory wh:category_Natural ;
    wh:hasComponent wh:component_1758-001,
        wh:component_1758-002 ;
    wh:hasCriterion wh:criterion_viii ;
    wh:hasImage wh:image_284074afb23ac9d7,
        wh:image_3c181724026e9d11,
