wh:site_1759 a wh:WorldHeritageSite ;
    rdfs:label "مجمعات مدافن نخبة شعب شيونغنو"@ar,
        "The Cemetery Complexes of the Xiongnu Nobility"@en,
        "Los conjuntos funerarios de la nobleza xiongnu"@es,
        "Les ensembles funéraires de la noblesse xiongnu"@fr,
        "Погребальные комплексы знати хунну"@ru,
        "匈奴贵族墓葬群"@zh ;
    wh:areaHectares 3409.0 ;
    wh:belongsToRegion wh:region_APA ;
    wh:description "This serial property comprises eight funerary complexes of the Xiongnu nobility. The Xiongnu, ancient predecessors of a confederation of Eurasian nomadic peoples, established a powerful empire between the 3rd century BCE and the 2nd century CE that eventually occupied a vast territory centred on what is now Mongolia. They interacted with the Han Chinese and controlled nodes along the main parts of the Steppe Route of the Silk Roads. Among the numerous archaeological remains found across the former expanse of the empire, their cemeteries provide important insights into Xiongnu burial culture. The main burials, notable for their substantial size and depth, are accompanied by satellite graves marked by small ring-shaped mounds, as well as sacrificial structures consisting of alignments of paired stones. A wide range of artefacts found in the tombs, including horse tack and chariots as well as weapons, tools and food for the afterlife, provides a window into the Xiongnu way of life and social structure. The tombs of the Xiongnu nobility highlight the engineering and organisational skills of this predominantly nomadic culture."@en ;
    wh:hasCategory wh:category_Cultural ;
    wh:hasComponent wh:component_1759-001,
        wh:component_1759-002,
        wh:component_1759-003,
        wh:component_1759-004,
