wh:site_1808 a wh:WorldHeritageSite ;
    rdfs:label "منظر الهجرة في بوما-باندينجيلو"@ar,
        "Boma–Badingilo Migratory Landscape"@en,
        "Paisaje migratorio de Boma-Badingilo"@es,
        "Paysage migratoire de Boma–Badingilo"@fr,
        "Миграционный ландшафт Бома — Бадингило"@ru,
        "博马—巴丁吉罗迁徙景观"@zh ;
    wh:areaHectares 11236800.0 ;
    wh:belongsToRegion wh:region_AFR ;
    wh:description "The property comprises Boma National Park, Badingilo National Park, and the surrounding floodplain and savanna landscapes in South Sudan. Located at the interface between the Sudd wetland system and the East Sudanian Savanna, it encompasses an extensive mosaic of seasonally flooded grasslands, wetlands, floodplains, woodlands, and savannas shaped by seasonal rainfall and flood dynamics. These interconnected ecosystems sustain one of the world’s largest remaining terrestrial mammal migrations, with an estimated six million White-eared Kob, Tiang, and Mongalla Gazelle moving across a vast and largely unimpeded landscape in response to changing environmental conditions. The site also reflects important cultural, and linguistic diversity of the region with the Murle, Dinka, Nuer, Anyuak and Toposa and other ethnic groups who have been custodians of this landscape through herding, farming and fishing for centuries and even millennia. The property also supports important populations of numerous large mammal species, including Nile Lechwe, African Savanna Elephant, African Wild Dog, Lion, Leopard, Northeastern Africa Cheetah, and Beisa Oryx, as well as significant assemblages of waterbirds, raptors, and migratory bird species. Its exceptional scale, ecological connectivity, habitat diversity, and relative intactness sustain ecological and biological processes that have become increasingly rare globally."@en ;
    wh:hasCategory wh:category_Natural ;
